file C:\Users\dalbrecht\Research
    Projects\ManyDaughters\ManyDaughters_DataPreprocessing/data/derived_confi
    > dential/95%-sample/h1_dataset.dta saved

. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
. 
.         *NOTICE 2: We do not include "urban/rural" nor "religious_freq", as s
> tated in the PAP, because of the high number of missing observations found
. 
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
.         
. /*      
> We construct the outcome variable, gei_std, as the difference in housework (h
> rs_chores) done by the male and female partners in the household and normaliz
> ing it by the total number of hours of housework in the household. The variab
> le is then standardized (mean = 0, standard deviation = 1) and the signed is 
> flipped so that higher values represent higher equality.
> 
> It is important to note that in households where the man already works more t
> han the woman, gender equality increases when the man works less. Hence, if t
> he hypothesis that having a daughter leads to more gender equality is true, f
> or these households it means that men would work less.
> 
> We estimate a linear regression model where the primary independent variable 
> is an indicator for whether the respondent has a daughter. The specification 
> controls for a rich set of covariates: average age of the couple, highest yea
> rs of education, income, nationality of the respondent, marital status, surve
> y year, and the age of the child. The coefficient on the daughter variable is
>  interpreted as the marginal effect of having a daughter (versus a son) on th
> e gender equality index gei_std, expressed in standard deviation units.
> 
> The sample is restricted to individuals with partners of the opposite sex (to
>  minimize the cases of gender selection of the child) and exactly one child. 
> Since the outcome variable is asked every year in the period 2000-2020, we ke
> ep only one observation per person by keeping the first valid observation for
>  each individual.
> 
> */      
.  
.  
.  
.         global controls_h3 "years_edu_max age_mean_hh age_child married  ln_i
> ncome i.syear i.nationality"

. 
.         *h3 -> we use data from the first year a MAN answers the pli0043_h qu
> estion to build the "GEI" between 2000-2020 
.         reg gei_std daughter $controls_h3  if sample==1&filter_year_sample_h3
> ==1 , robust 

